## All Vec Methods in One Place

Here's every Vec method you need for daily use, with real scenarios for each.

### Check If Empty: .is_empty()

```xiom
if stack.is_empty() {
  io.println("Nothing to undo!");
}
else {
  match stack.pop() {
    Some(v) -> io.println(v);
    None -> {}
  }
}
```

Always check `.is_empty()` before operations that depend on having data.

### Count Items: .len()

```xiom
io.println("Queue has " + string.int_to_str(queue.len()) + " people waiting");
```

### Build a Stack Using Vec

A stack is a LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) data structure. Vec with push/pop IS a stack:

```xiom
fn push(stack: Vec[Int], val: Int) { stack.push(val); }
fn peek(stack: Vec[Int]) -> Option[Int] { return stack.get(stack.len() - 1); }
```

### Full Program: Browser History

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let history: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();

  io.println(history.is_empty());

  history.push("google.com");
  history.push("wikipedia.org");
  history.push("github.com");

  io.println(history.len());
  io.println(history.is_empty());

  match history.pop() {
    Some(page) -> io.println("Back from: " + page);
    None -> io.println("No history");
  }

  io.println(history.len());
  return 0;
}
```

